      "question": "My employer is transferring me overseas for 4 years. Can I keep buying in my NISA while abroad?",
      "answer": {
        "usage": {
          "input": 56335,
          "cacheRead": 21876,
          "cacheWrite": 0,
          "output": 1048
        },
        "answer": "**No.** Buying new units is not allowed while you are abroad. Because your employer is transferring you, you can file a continuation notice (継続適用届出書) with your broker by the day before departure. Your existing holdings then stay tax-free, and you can still sell them. New purchases, including dividend reinvestment, are not allowed. Your 4 years fits inside the window, which runs to 31 December of the year five years after the notice. File a return notice (帰国届出書) when you come back, and purchases can resume from that day. Brokers set earlier internal deadlines, and some do not support the continuation notice at all. Exit-tax subjects (about ¥100 million or more in assets) are excluded. Check with your broker now. This is general information, not tax advice.",
        "verdict": "no",
